 other functions designed for  cell based assays not only for cell  based assays but this is in my  experience the most popular application  field for these so-called environmental  control features so researchers  typically want to do long term cell  growth studies and in this context the  heating function and the ability of the  instrument to maintain a target  temperature of 37 degrees is most useful  but it's not only a heating function  that the spark possesses it's in fact  the first reader on the market that not  only has the ability to heat the  measurement chamber but also permits  lowering the operation temperature below  ambient the minimum temperature that can  be reached with this system at the  moment is 18 degrees so that's well  below the normal room temperature level  it may not actually be like refrigerator  conditions but it meets the requirements  of many temperature sensitive probes and  samples and in addition it helps to  maintain a constant temperature during  long term kinetic measurements by  actively preventing instrument heating  so how is this cooling function  accomplished the spark reader when  equipped with the so-called t cool  module is a two component system it uses  a special external and internal part the  external water cooling unit chills the  water outside of the instrument and this  is then used to cool  or as a cooling medium which is pumped  back into the readers internal cooling  module to cool the air inside the reader  and the air that has been warmed up  during operation is pumped back to the  cooling module to the external part  cooled again and then returned to the  reader and this cycle serves to maintain  a specified temperature if needed below  ampion ambient temperature even for  long-term measurement periods so even  for several days if needed so why is a  constant  sorry yeah here we go why is a constant  s a temperature so important well first  of all scientific studies done in  different labs in different parts of the  world should still have the same outcome  if they are performed in the same way a  scientist in Stockholm Sweden should be  able to reproduce the results of another  scientist in San Diego California  when using the same sample same s a  chemistry and the same experimental  setup the components of the experiment  should react and behave in the same way  no matter in which climate conditions  the SA is performed in reality this is  hardly ever the case reagents and other  si components particularly enzymes and  other temperature sensitive components  have temperature Optima so that means  best working conditions and they may  lose a significant portion of their  activity when used outside this optimal  operation temperature or the target  temperature range so for repre do Segel  and comparable results it is vital to  maintain a defined target temperature at  which the si is performed day by day in  addition the reaction kinetics shouldn't  be influenced by temperature increases  in the lab throughout the course of the  day so we all know in the mornings it  tends to be a little cooler than at  and in the afternoon especially during  these long summer days hot summer days  the temperature in the lab can really  rise significantly and all of this  shouldn't influence the dynamics of your  essays so using any type of temperature  stabilizing function like the T cool  module will make your results much more  reliable and reproducible one of the  first scientists to find out about the  susceptibility of chemical and  biological reactions to temperature was  Jacobus Henricus vant Hoff a Dutch  physical chemist and the winner of the  first Nobel Prize in Chemistry his  pioneering work helped found the modern  theory of chemical affinity equilibrium  chemical kinetics and chemical  thermodynamics he stipulated that  enzymatic activity is directly dependent  on temperature and the corresponding  equation that is still the basis for  these findings is named after him the so  called vant Hoff equation he found that  for example the speed of a chemical  reaction is increased by up to 12% if  the temperature is increased by only 1  degree Celsius which can lead to a  variation in the final measurement  results of even 10 to 20% so really a  significant change just based on  fluctuations in the temperature level  and his findings can be easily confirmed  in many modern day scientific  applications as we can see here on this  slide the signal uniformity of replicate  samples can drastically vary when an SI  is performed at different temperature  levels the green line is how it should  be you could say a constant temperature  throughout the entire measurement period  means lowest possible cv lowest possible  results variation so this is what every  researcher should aim for providing a  constant temperature  for the SA set up throughout the entire  measurement period in contrast if the  temperature increased by 4 or 8% as was  the case in this example experiment here  you can see a massive results change so  a massive increase in the signal itself  but also a massive increase in the CB  the coefficient of variation of the  replicate samples particularly the  reaction surface from the temperature  increase during the readout which as you  can see leads to an accelerating slope  of the signal curve that becomes more  profound the higher the temperature gets  so this is exactly what the T cool  function of the spark reader is intended  for maintaining a constant temperature  even if the ambient temperature in the  lab Rises throughout the analysis period  and in addition having the option to  control temperature inside your either  even below ambient opens up  possibilities for new applications for  example you can mimic the water  temperature conditions for marine  organisms which usually like a  surrounding temperature of 18 to 20  degrees so that makes it an ideal tool  to study the responses of marine species  to climate changes for